\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A flat characteristic-two kernel exceeds the cohomology}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Take trivial scalar links on the cellular #{2\times2} torus
over #{\mathbb F_2}, with the perfect counting pairing. The
total cochain space has dimension #{16}. Write
#{a_i=T_i-\bbI}. Since #{T_i^2=\bbI},
#{T_i^{-1}=T_i} and #{a_i^2=0}. Thus
##{\extD=\sum_{i=1}^2(e_i\wedge+(e_i\cdot))a_i.}
The exterior insertion and contraction relations give
#{(e_i\wedge+(e_i\cdot))^2=\bbI}; the two distinct-direction
operators anticommute. The #{a_i} commute, so
#{\extD^2=a_1^2+a_2^2=0}.}

\p{It follows that #{\im\extD\subseteq\ker\extD}.
Rank-nullity on the #{16}-dimensional space therefore gives
#{\dim\ker\extD\ge8}. But \ref{fdip-00SW} with trivial
holonomies computes total cohomology dimension #{2^2=4}.
Perfectness and flatness thus do not replace positivity in
\ref{fdip-00SX}. The earlier example in \ref{fdip-00OM}
concerns the counting Laplacian; here the mismatch is for
#{\extD} itself.}
